// Pull request author: saakovv
// Change: new rule
// Title: Linux Privileged Docker Container Execution
// Sigma ID: c9f4a7e2-2d8b-4a31-c3e9-1b5f8d4a7c20
// Status: experimental | Level: high
// Author: Ivan Saakov
// Date: 2026-05-10 | Modified: -
// ATT&CK: privilege-escalation, T1611
// Description: Detects execution of docker run or docker exec with the --privileged flag. A privileged
//   container has full access to the host kernel, all devices, and bypasses Docker namespace
//   isolation. Attackers exploit this to mount the host filesystem, load kernel modules, access raw
//   devices, and escape the container to gain root on the underlying host. The --restart=always flag
//   combined with --privileged additionally establishes persistent access.
// False positives:
//   - Legitimate use of privileged containers by platform or infrastructure teams
//   - Container runtime components that require privileged mode for system operations
// References:
//   - https://docs.docker.com/engine/reference/run/#runtime-privilege-and-linux-capabilities
//   - https://www.trendmicro.com/en_us/research/19/l/why-running-a-privileged-container-in-docker-is-a-bad-idea.html
// Sigma rule: https://github.com/SigmaHQ/sigma/blob/e5bac28da3655c33390db7ef1503db8cd3babb5f/rules/linux/process_creation/proc_creation_lnx_docker_privileged.yml
// License: Detection Rule License (DRL) 1.1 - https://github.com/SigmaHQ/Detection-Rule-License
// Converted by sigma2kql (target: microsoft_xdr, pySigma 1.5.1, pySigma-backend-kusto 1.0.1)
// Validation: passed (Kusto.Language, Defender schema)
// Machine-generated and not tested against real telemetry. Verify before use as a detection.

DeviceProcessEvents
| where (FolderPath endswith "/docker" and ProcessCommandLine contains "--privileged") and (ProcessCommandLine contains " run " or ProcessCommandLine contains " exec ")
